  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 311 PM CDT Sat Sep 12 2026\n\nAn upper shortwave trough is currently sliding across the Central \nPlains with an additional shortwave over the Lower Mississippi \nValley. Upper troughing remains over Canada with zonal flow aloft \nover the Northern Plains. A deeper upper trough is developing off \nthe coast of the Pacific Northwest. At the surface, a cold front is \ndraped across portions of central KS then continues to curve \ndown into the Texas Panhandle. An outflow boundary has moved \nthrough northern Oklahoma into central Oklahoma. There are \nongoing storms in southern Nebraska and portions of northeast \nKansas due to 700mb WAA. Activity continues to develop in far \nsoutheast Kansas and push east/southeast. \n\nFor later this afternoon, storms are expected to develop in the \neastern portion of the Oklahoma Panhandle where the outflow boundary \nmeets the cold front. These storms are anticipated to move \neast/northeast into southern Kansas as we move into this evening \naffecting portions of south-central and southeast Kansas late this \nevening into tonight. Severe storms are likely with this activity as \ninstability in this area is progged to be greater than 2500 J/kg \nwith 35-40 kts of effective shear. This combined with steep mid-\nlevel lapse rates could be capable of producing golfball size hail \nwith the strongest storms. Damaging wind gusts around 60 mph are \nalso possible given DCAPE values greater than 1300 J/kg. Additional \nstorm development is expected across the area tonight as the LLJ \nramps up coupled with mid-level WAA. There is still uncertainty with \nexact location for storms but recent guidance suggests scattered \nchances will be possible across much of eastern Kansas during the \novernight hours into early Sunday morning. Showers and storms are \nexpected to continue to move east Sunday morning with chances \nstarting to diminish late Sunday morning. \n\nFor Sunday afternoon, mostly dry conditions are forecast with cloud \ncover dissipating. Recent model guidance suggests the frontal \nboundary lifting north as a warm front, returning southerly flow to \nour area. Above normal 850mb temperatures are progged especially \nacross southern Kansas, leading to another day of above normal \ntemperatures. Highs are forecast to be in the upper 80s to mid 90s \nwith portions of south-central Kansas in the upper 90s. Heat indices \nwill be near 100 especially in southern Kansas Sunday afternoon to \nearly evening. Hot conditions will continue into Monday across the \nwhole area as well above normal 850mb temperatures continue. High \ntemperatures will be in the upper 90s to potentially the low 100s \nwith heat indices following suit. Guidance remains consistent with \nan upper trough propagating towards the Northern Plains by Monday \nafternoon. This will increase the pressure gradient in the northern \nand central Plains, resulting in breezy southerly winds in Kansas \nMonday afternoon. \n\nFor late Monday night to early Tuesday morning, the aforementioned \nupper trough will continue to track east, pushing a cold front down \nthrough Nebraska into northern Kansas. Storms are likely to develop \nalong it increasing rain chances late Monday night. The front looks \nto stall out across central KS Tuesday afternoon resulting in a \nspread of temperatures. Highs are currently forecast to range from \n70s in far northern KS to the upper 90s in southern Kansas. \nAdditional rainfall chances will be possible through much of the \nweek as embedded weak perturbations continue to slide across the \nregion. Highs for Wednesday have trended warmer than previously \nthought with highs ranging from the 80s in central KS to the mid 90s \nin southern KS. Slightly cooler temperatures are progged to arrive \nfor Thursday and Friday with highs mainly in the 80s across the \narea.",
